  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nToday and tomorrow, we remain embedded between an upper level \ntrough over the Great Basin and ridge over the Gulf. The moisture \nassociated with these patterns remain just to our northwest and \nsoutheast keeping us in a drier region. Any rainfall amounts \nimpacting our area would just scrape Hudspeth County. Otherwise, \nwidespread dry, seasonable conditions remain in effect. \nTemperatures east of the Rio Grande run above normal while \ntemperatures west of the river run slightly below normal because \nof the two distinct air masses we are currently embedded between.\n\nMain headline for this forecast is a strong backdoor cold front\narriving Thursday night/Friday morning. This will be our first\nsignificant cool air intrusion of the cool season. Strong east\nwinds and scattered rain showers will accompany it, leading to a\nstretch of gloomy, blustery conditions Friday through the\nweekend.\n\nThursday afternoon is when we notice a distinct wind shift as the\nupper level trough produces a closed low pressure system \ndeveloping to our west. This will invoke a strong easterly flow \nlate Thursday evening and through the day Friday primarily \nimpacting the western slopes of our area mountains. Temperature \nadvecting out of our northeast with this front will be colder \nresulting in a temperature decrease of 10 to 15 degrees. These \nwind gusts over the Sacs and Bootheel will likely exceed 50 mph \nwith sustained winds over 35 mph holding out over Hudspeth and El \nPaso counties through Friday morning. Moving into the weekend, the\nwestern regions of our CWA remain breezy through Saturday \nmorning. Southerly moisture advects northward by week's end though\ncurrent long range runs are not as pronounced and seem to scrape \nthe Gila, eastern Hudspeth, and the Sacs once again. Since this \nmoisture is moving into a noticeably cooler area, the possibility \nof a rain/snow mix in the highest elevations cannot be ruled out. \nWidespread lowland rain showers dominate the GFS on Saturday, but \nthe EC keeps this moisture confined to the western half of the CWA\nduring the early hours of our weekend.",
